{%- comment -%}
  Product card — native Shopify port of app.js productCardHTML().
  Usage: {% render 'product-card', product: product %}

  Reads optional metafields (custom.badge, reviews.rating / custom.rating,
  reviews.rating_count / custom.review_count) with graceful fallbacks so a
  bare product still renders.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign badge = product.metafields.custom.badge.value
  assign badge_class = product.metafields.custom.badge_class.value | default: 'badge-best'
  # "New" never renders. 1,155 of the 1,308 products in the catalogue carry it,
  # so it marked nothing out — a badge that is on 88% of the shelf is just
  # decoration. It used to be suppressed per-caller with hide_new, which meant
  # the same product looked new in one row and not in another. Dropped here
  # instead, once, for every surface. Sale and Bestseller still show.
  if badge == 'New'
    assign badge = blank
  endif
  if badge == blank
    if product.compare_at_price > product.price
      assign badge = 'Sale'
      assign badge_class = 'badge-sale'
    elsif product.tags contains 'bestseller'
      assign badge = 'Bestseller'
      assign badge_class = 'badge-best'
    endif
  endif

  assign rating = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value.rating | default: product.metafields.custom.rating.value
  assign review_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | default: product.metafields.custom.review_count.value

  # Quick Add posts to the real cart, so it needs a variant id. Every product
  # in the catalogue is single-variant today; the size check keeps the button
  # honest if a multi-size product is ever added, sending the shopper to the
  # product page to choose rather than silently adding the first size.
  assign card_variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign card_title = product.title | escape

  # Spec line under the name — the size and who it is for, which is what a
  # shopper scanning a row actually compares. Falls back through the variant
  # title, then the product type, so a card without metafields still says
  # something rather than leaving a gap.
  assign card_size = product.metafields.custom.size.value
  if card_size == blank and card_variant.title != 'Default Title'
    assign card_size = card_variant.title
  endif
  assign card_gender = product.metafields.custom.gender.value
  capture card_spec
    if card_size != blank
      echo card_size
      if card_gender != blank
        echo ' · '
      endif
    endif
    if card_gender != blank
      echo card_gender
    endif
  endcapture
  assign card_spec = card_spec | strip
  if card_spec == blank
    assign card_spec = product.type
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The whole card is clickable through one real link, the product name,
  stretched over the card by .prod-link::after in theme.css. The card used to
  be a div with onclick="location.href=…", which navigates from JavaScript.
  That meant ⌘/Ctrl-click, middle-click and right-click → Open in new tab did
  nothing, there was no URL preview on hover, and crawlers saw no link. The
  wishlist and Quick Add buttons are raised above the stretched link, so
  they still work on their own.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  The data-ga-* attributes feed GA4's view_item_list and select_item. They are
  discrete attributes rather than one JSON blob because a collection page
  renders 24 of these and Liquid's json filter on a title containing a quote
  would need escaping in an HTML attribute context — four small strings are
  cheaper to emit and impossible to get wrong. analytics.js reads them; with
  no analytics configured they cost four attributes and nothing else.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="prod-card fade-up" data-handle="{{ product.handle }}"{% if card_variant %} data-variant="{{ card_variant.id }}"{% endif %}
     data-pid="{{ product.id }}"
     data-ga-name="{{ card_title }}"
     data-ga-brand="{{ product.vendor | escape }}"
     data-ga-cat="{{ product.type | default: 'Perfume' | escape }}"
     data-ga-price="{{ product.price | divided_by: 100.0 }}">
  <div class="prod-img-wrap">
    {%- if product.featured_image -%}
      <img class="prod-img" src="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 600 }}" alt="{{ product.title | escape }}" loading="lazy" width="600" height="600">
      {%- comment -%}
        Hover image: the next image after the featured one, laid exactly over
        it and faded in on hover — replacing the old 5% zoom of the same photo.
        In a sample of the catalogue the second image was the clean white-
        background shot 83% of the time. It is decorative (the product is
        already named by the first image's alt), so it is hidden from screen
        readers. Products with a single image simply keep that image.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- for img in product.images -%}
        {%- if img.id != product.featured_image.id -%}
          <img class="prod-img prod-img-hover" src="{{ img | image_url: width: 600 }}" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" width="600" height="600">
          {%- break -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- else -%}
      <img class="prod-img" src="{{ 'prod-bouquet-oud.png' | asset_url }}" alt="{{ product.title | escape }}" loading="lazy">
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if badge != blank -%}<div class="prod-badge {{ badge_class }}">{{ badge }}</div>{%- endif -%}
    <div class="prod-actions">
      <button type="button" class="pa-btn pa-wish"
              data-wish="{{ product.handle }}"
              data-wish-label="{{ card_title }}"
              aria-pressed="false"
              title="Save to wishlist"
              aria-label="Save {{ card_title }} to wishlist"
              onclick="event.stopPropagation();TFSWishlist.toggle(this.dataset.wish, this.dataset.wishLabel)"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">favorite</span></button>
      {%- if product.variants.size > 1 -%}
        <a class="pa-btn quick-add" href="{{ product.url }}" onclick="event.stopPropagation()"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">shopping_bag</span>Choose Size</a>
      {%- elsif card_variant and card_variant.available -%}
        <button type="button" class="pa-btn quick-add"
                onclick="event.stopPropagation();addLiveToCart({{ card_variant.id }}, {{ product.title | json | escape }})"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">shopping_bag</span>Quick Add</button>
      {%- else -%}
        <button type="button" class="pa-btn quick-add" disabled><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">shopping_bag</span>Sold Out</button>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="prod-info">
    {%- if product.vendor != blank -%}<div class="prod-brand">{{ product.vendor }}</div>{%- endif -%}
    <div class="prod-name"><a class="prod-link" href="{{ product.url }}">{{ product.title }}</a></div>
    {%- if card_spec != blank -%}<div class="prod-spec">{{ card_spec }}</div>{%- endif -%}
    <div class="prod-rating"><div class='jdgm-widget jdgm-preview-badge' data-id='{{ product.id }}'>{{ product.metafields.judgeme.badge }}</div></div>
    <div class="prod-price">
      {%- comment -%}
        product.price is the range's low end, so on a multi-size product the
        card quoted a price the PDP would not open on. "From" makes the card
        honest without reordering anyone's variants.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if product.price_min != product.price_max -%}<span class="price-from">From</span>{%- endif -%}
      <span class="price-now">{{ product.price | money_with_currency }}</span>
      {%- if product.compare_at_price > product.price -%}
        <span class="price-was">{{ product.compare_at_price | money_with_currency }}</span>
        <span class="price-save">Save {{ product.compare_at_price | minus: product.price | times: 100.0 | divided_by: product.compare_at_price | round }}%</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
